From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicolas Villar <15879262+nvillar@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 02:19:44 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] Duo: launch apps fullscreen while the two panel window policy
 is on

The Duo-DE launch options ask for a freeform window with explicit launch bounds on a tablet sized profile
(allow_freeform_mode_tablet, on by default). With the framework's two panel policy on, the framework opens a launched
app fullscreen in the panel that was tapped; a freeform request with bounds fights that routing. While the policy is
active, launch fullscreen and leave the panel choice to the framework. With the policy off nothing changes.

"Active" is the window manager's state for this boot, published by the framework as sys.duode.panels.active
(frameworks_base "Duo: publish whether the two panel window policy is active"), not the persist.*.phh.duo.panels
switches, which only take effect after a reboot. Without that framework patch the property is absent and Launcher3
keeps its previous behaviour.

Not built or run on a device yet; part of the M1 gate prototype.

Co-authored-by: Copilot App <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../launcher3/util/ActivityOptionsWrapper.java       |  4 ++++
 src/com/android/launcher3/util/DuoPanels.java        | 12 ++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/com/android/launcher3/util/ActivityOptionsWrapper.java b/src/com/android/launcher3/util/ActivityOptionsWrapper.java
index 1a4fc5141d..5b58fedd68 100644
--- a/src/com/android/launcher3/util/ActivityOptionsWrapper.java
+++ b/src/com/android/launcher3/util/ActivityOptionsWrapper.java
@@ -90,6 +90,10 @@ public class ActivityOptionsWrapper {
             }
         }
 
+        // Duo two panel policy: the framework opens the app fullscreen in the panel that was tapped. A freeform request
+        // with launch bounds would fight that routing, so the freeform preference does not apply while the policy is on.
+        if (DuoPanels.panelPolicyEnabled()) mode = "fullscreen";
+
         if (mode.equals("fullscreen")) {
             windowMode = 1;
         } else {
diff --git a/src/com/android/launcher3/util/DuoPanels.java b/src/com/android/launcher3/util/DuoPanels.java
index f2679b66d6..f68f011dae 100644
--- a/src/com/android/launcher3/util/DuoPanels.java
+++ b/src/com/android/launcher3/util/DuoPanels.java
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 package com.android.launcher3.util;
 
 import android.os.Build;
+import android.os.SystemProperties;
 
 import com.android.internal.policy.DuoPanelGeometry;
 
@@ -45,6 +46,17 @@ public final class DuoPanels {
         return halves(widthPx, heightPx) != null;
     }
 
+    /**
+     * True when the framework built the two panel window hierarchy for this boot. DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider publishes
+     * sys.duode.panels.active when it creates the default display; the persist.*.phh.duo.panels switches themselves
+     * only take effect at the next boot, so reading them here would disagree with the window manager after a toggle
+     * without reboot.
+     */
+    public static boolean panelPolicyEnabled() {
+        if (DuoPanelGeometry.forDevice(Build.DEVICE) == null) return false;
+        return SystemProperties.getBoolean("sys.duode.panels.active", false);
+    }
+
     /** True when x is on the right screen; a touch on the hinge goes to the nearer screen. */
     public static boolean isRightHalf(int[][] halves, float x) {
         return x >= (halves[0][2] + halves[1][0]) / 2f;
-- 
